 ],
 "literal": "Look there, that indeed! This, what shall I say? / On the strong branch stayed, the dark-flocked birds, / because they united, of those who are separated, in the mind, / sweet voice to coo, having heard, he who left, / the stranger, here if he comes, like a flower bud, / thick hair do not adorn, / me also do not touch, I will say indeed!",
 "translation": "Look there! That is it! What shall I say to this? If that stranger who left me comes back here, having heard the sweet voices of the dark-feathered birds perched on strong branches cooing because they have united—voices that pierce the hearts of those who are separated—I will surely say to him: 'Do not adorn my thick hair with flower buds, and do not touch me!'",
 "modern": "அங்கே பார்! அதுதான்! இதற்கு நான் என்ன சொல்வேன்? வலிமையான கிளையில் அமர்ந்திருக்கும் கரிய கூட்டமாகிய பறவைகள், தாம் ஒன்று சேர்ந்த மகிழ்ச்சியால், பிரிந்திருப்பவர்களின் நெஞ்சில் வருத்தத்தை உண்டாக்கும்படி இனிமையான குரலில் கூவுவதைக் கேட்டு, நம்மை விட்டுப் பிரிந்து சென்ற அந்த அந்நியர் இப்போது இங்கே வந்தால், 'மலரும் மொட்டுகளைக் கொண்டு என் அடர்ந்த கூந்தலை அலங்கரிக்காதே; என்னைத் தொடவும் செய்யாதே' என்று நான் உறுதியாகக் கூறுவேன்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Mullai (forest) landscape, where the heroine is waiting for her husband's return during the rainy season. Seeing the birds happily united in pairs on the strong branches of trees, she feels the pain of separation, as their sweet cooing sounds like a torment to those who are lonely. She refers to her husband as a 'stranger' (ஏதிலாளர்) because he left her behind. In her playful anger (ஊடல்), she tells her friend that if he returns now, she will refuse to let him adorn her thick hair with flower buds and will forbid him from touching her. The imagery of the united birds contrasts sharply with her own solitude, highlighting her longing and feigned resentment.",
